4 stones (G4-G7) which is open at G3 and G8, also an Open 4 and a guaranteed win.\n*   **White (O)** needs to either block all of these immediate threats (which is impossible with a single passive stone as they do not overlap) or play a **forcing move** that threatens an immediate win, compelling Black to defend.\n\n**Winning Moves Evaluation:**\n*   **Candidate 1: G6 or H4.** These are defensive blocks. Blocking G6 stops the vertical threat but leaves the diagonal threat (H4/D8) open. Blocking H4 stops the diagonal threat but leaves the vertical threat (G6) open. Since Black has two distinct lethal threats, a passive defense leads to a loss.\n*   **Candidate 2: K8.** White has a diagonal line of three stones: H5, I6, and J7. Playing at **K8** extends this line to form a **4-in-a-row** (H5-I6-J7-K8).\n    *   This is a "Check". It creates an immediate threat of winning on the next turn by playing at L9 (forming 5).\n    *   Black **must** block at L9 immediately to survive.\n    *   By forcing Black\'s response, White seizes the initiative. This is the only move that prevents Black from executing their winning attacks on this turn.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe only viable strategy for White to survive and potentially win is to play the forcing move at K8. This turns the table by creating a lethal threat that takes precedence over Black\'s current setups.\n\nEvaluation: White has a diagonal "sleeping three" (H5-I6-J7) blocked at G4 but open towards K8. Playing K8 creates a "Blocked Four" (four-in-a-row), which is an immediate lethal threat.\n\nReason: Black has multiple winning threats (an open 3 on the diagonal and a broken 3 on the vertical). Passive defense cannot stop both. White must take the initiative by playing K8, which forms a 4-in-a-row. This forces Black to defend at L9, preventing Black from playing their winning moves and giving White the attack.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "K", "row": 8 }'}
